{"type":"assistant","uuid":"01a1b6f8-1713-7597-98ba-9e9cc0735713","session_id":"01a1b6f5-80e5-79d6-a660-1b272ac4a004","message":{"id":"01a1b6f81713759798ba9e9a8137f08c","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Excellent. This gives a lot of insight into the CI runner:\n\n- It extracts \"checks\": id/title pairs. Note ids: \"0\" for 辅助函数, \"1\" for 通用要求, \"A1\" for \"-01 完整运行\", \"-02 完整运行的清单\"... So the parser is splitting on `## ` AND `### `, producing title as text after heading marker, and the id as the first token before a hyphen? Actually for `### A1-01 完整运行` it parsed id \"A1\" and title \"-01 完整运行\". For `## 0. 辅助函数（先执行一次）` → id \"0\", title \"辅助函数（先执行一次）\" (dot and number). For `## 1. 通用要求` → id \"1\", title \"通用要求\".\n\nSo the parser: for headings of level 2 or 3, id = first whitespace-delimited token stripped of trailing punctuation (`.`), title = rest.\n\nCrucially: each check's body is the whole fenced block(s) until next heading, executed as a single script. And for `## 1. 通用要求` body contains all `### V-01 ...` sections — so it executed the helper functions AND... wait, id \"1\" output was the TAP test output. Hmm, `## 1. 通用要求` executed and produced the select test output? That looks like it concatenated all the fenced blocks in the section (V-01..V-05), last command being `node --test test/unit/select.test.mjs; $LASTEXITCODE`. Yes! So the parser concatenates all fenced code blocks within a section (until next heading of same-or-higher level?) Hmm.\n\nActually maybe parser splits by `### ` when present? No—`## 1. 通用要求` check ran commands from V-01...V-05, which means the section body extended across `###` subheadings. So parser splits ONLY on `## ` (level 2), and `###` are just part of the body! And id \"1\" is `## 1. 通用要求`.\n\nWait but then where do ids \"A1\"/titles \"-01 完整运行\" come from? `## 2. AC-001：运行器、完整与过滤模式` would be id 2. Hmm, but results show A1-01 etc. So it does split on `###` too.\n\nThen how did section \"1\" include all V-01..V-05? Because `###` headings in that section... Hmm, V-01 has a fence; V-02 has a fence... maybe parser splits on headings that are followed by a fenced block? No.\n\nAlternative: parser splits on `###` for checks, and for `## 1. 通用要求` it also created a check whose body is text up to next `##`... but that would duplicate.\n\nLook at result ids: \"0\", \"1\", \"A1\", \"A1\"... So there are checks named 0 and 1 (the `##` sections), then A1-01... Wait — maybe the parser's check detection: a heading line matching `^#{2,3}\\s` starts a check; id = first token; but a `##` heading whose *own body* contains `###` headings... no.\n\nHold on. Maybe titles like \"1\" come from `## 1. 通用要求`, and since that section has no fence directly after, the parser... produced TAP output? The output for id 1 was TAP select test output (V-05's last command `node --test test/unit/select.test.mjs; $LASTEXITCODE` → lastLine \"0\"). So yes, the body of check \"1\" = all fences from V-01..V-05. That means the parser splits on `###` only when ... hmm.\n\nMaybe the parser splits the document by headings of the form `^## ` first, and within each `##` section, if there are `###` subheadings, it splits further — but for section 1, it also... no.\n\nAnother theory: the runner parses the *first* fenced block after each heading? No—the output of \"1\" included the whole set.\n\nLet me reconsider: maybe the parser is line-based: it walks lines; when it sees a heading `^#{2,3}`, it starts a new check with id=first token, title=rest, and collects all fenced code until the next heading of *level ≤ 2*? No, because then A1-01..A1-13 would each be separate (they were, since ids A1 appear 13 times).\n\nHmm wait — maybe ids \"A1\" duplicated 13 times because id is derived from the **file**, i.e. section prefix... Hmm \"A1\" for all A1-xx. And \"0\"/\"1\" for `## 0.` and `## 1.`.\n\nOK here's a cleaner theory: the parser treats headings of level 2 AND 3 as checks, but when a level-3 check has the same code as the enclosing level-2 (i.e., `## 1. 通用要求` vs `### V-01` — no match)...\n\nAlternatively the parser might use regex `^##+\\s+(\\S+?)[\\s.]*(.*)$`... \n\nLet's think about \"one check per '## ' section\" from the note. That says: exactly one check per `##` section. So the parser splits on `##`, and each `##` section is one check. That explains ids: \"0\", \"1\"?? Then A1-01 would not be separate checks. Unless... the note is the orchestrator's advice to make each check a `##` section (i.e. use `## X-NN title` instead of `###`). And the parser DOES accept `###` as well, but splitting differently.\n\nUgh. Maybe the parser splits on the *last* heading level present? Hmm.\n\nLet me look at ci-results.json for task 001 fully (it's committed) to derive the exact mapping of ids/titles to sections. Then I can infer.\n\nActually, importantly: task 001's ci-results shows 42 total checks and pass 36 — for a doc with 5 `##` numbered sections + ... let's count `###` headings: V-01..V-05 (5), A1-01..A1-13 (13), A2-01..A2-04 (4), A3-01..A3-06 (6), A4-01..A4-08 (8), A74-01..A74-08 (8), T-01..T-03 (3) = 47. Plus `## 0.` and `## 1.` = 2 → 49. Total was 42. Hmm, 42... Let's see: maybe some `###` sections didn't produce results? Or the doc at that time (76e2b72, before b860131) had fewer.\n\nHmm, \"total\": 42. Let me check the actual ci-results.json list of titles.\n\nActually, the important question: what structure must MY acceptance.md have to be parsed correctly? The note tells me: one check per `## ` section, body = fenced block whose first line is the runnable command, followed by simple bullet assertions.\n\nHmm, that contradicts 001's `###` structure (\"exact structure that task 001 used\"). Unless the note's author (the orchestrator) is describing... hmm, \"reference: .collab/tasks/001/acceptance.md on main\" and \"Keep one check per '## ' section\". Since 001 uses `###` per check, maybe the note loosely means \"section\" = heading section (any level), and the runner splits on `###`.\n\nHon